  "content": {
    "id": "p14-cc-5-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "3",
    "prompt": "Explain why the following notation is not valid.\n\n$$f'(x^2)=2x.$$\n\nWhat does $f'(x^2)$ mean instead?",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "To differentiate $x^2$, write $\\dfrac{d}{dx}(x^2)=2x$. The expression $f'(x^2)$ evaluates the derivative function $f'$ at $x^2$; its value depends on $f$.",
      "worked": "The derivative of $x^2$ with respect to $x$ is written $\\dfrac{d}{dx}(x^2)=2x$. In $f'(x^2)$, $x^2$ is the input to the derivative function $f'$. Its value depends on the definition of $f$.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
